00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.940
So safety has always been the
number one goal for Departments

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.860
of Transportation.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.480
While fatality rates have
decreased over the years,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.060
in recent years,
we've seen an uptick

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.930
in the number of fatalities.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.620
So what's causing this change?

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.110
Well, the number of
vehicles on the road

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.190
are increasing faster
than the number of people.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.250
From 2016 to 2017,
we saw an increase

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.520
of 1.6% in the number
of registered vehicles,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.920
compared to a 0.8% increase
in the overall population.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.960
This is leading to more
congested and dangerous

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:55.460
roadways.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.980
And DOT budgets
aren't increasing.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.160
If anything, they're shrinking
while construction costs

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.010
are rising.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:09.588
To answer this
transportation problem,

00:02:09.588 --> 00:02:12.130
we shouldn't continue to do the
exact same thing we've always

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.963
done, which is
building more roads.

00:02:14.963 --> 00:02:16.630
While that's definitely
needed, and it's

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.220
something we'll
definitely do, there's

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.650
not enough space to
continue to do that.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.350
And also, it's
extremely costly, and it

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.290
takes years to see an
impact of congestion

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.430
for new construction.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:33.990
One way we could improve upon
and optimize traffic management

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:39.370
is by viewing the roadway as
a highway for information,

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.600
versus a highway that
carries vehicles.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.270
DOTs have spent decades
deploying traffic management

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:52.150
solutions that produce
massive volumes of data--

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:57.450
sidebar radars, toll
meters, weather stations,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.970
traffic cameras.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.580
Harnessing this data
from the roadway

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:08.300
will help us optimize traffic
both now and into the future.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.140
The good news is that the
data we need does exist.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.080
The not so great news is
that it generally resides

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.530
in very different
places and is generally

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.310
disconnected, leaving us with
a fragmented view, at best,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.910
of what's happening
on the roadways.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.110
And there's many different
transportation data

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:32.550
sources that exist, but, again,
they live in different places.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.290
Critical data that we could
take advantage of now,

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:41.170
such as INRIX, GIS data,
Amber Alerts, traffic cameras,

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.550
are all over the place--

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:48.005
and even key data sources,
like Waze and Google Maps.

00:03:48.005 --> 00:03:49.630
Without a clear
understanding of what's

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.780
happening on the
roadways, our communities

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.500
pay for mobility and
sometimes with their lives.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.980
But what if we could bring all
this data together and even

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.030
let data be the driver
for traffic management?

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.460
We could enhance
situational awareness,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.090
leveraging current
and historical data--

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.240
both situational data,
weather data, traffic data--

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.690
to respond to the traffic
incidents in near real-time,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:24.290
the moment they happen.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.600
We could coordinate response
across courtesy patrols,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.710
towing, maintenance, law
enforcement, emergency

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:37.510
response, to respond to an
incident based on where it is,

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.150
from community feedback.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.100
We could assess roadway
needs using data points

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.830
like traffic volume and
frequency, weather events,

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.850
community feedback,
to understand

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:56.680
where we need to do repairs in
a predictive way, on the roads.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.760
With all this data
brought together in a view

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.180
that you know best, an
interface such as Google Maps,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.420
you can harness the data to
answer some key questions,

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.810
things like, how has
traffic changed over time?

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.790
What alternate routes
can be utilized?

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.400
When is congestion
expected to peak?

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.940
And where are the most
trafficked roadways?

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.020
We can visualize this data with
Google Data Studio or partner

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.300
solutions from
Looker and Tableau.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.400
You can overlay your
insights on a real-time view,

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:29.390
using Google Maps.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.350
Once the data is
centrally organized

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.740
and available for both historic
and real-time analysis,

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.870
we can make
data-driven decisions,

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.540
helping to make roads
and drivers safer.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.790
So why did CDOT choose
Google Cloud to build

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.050
its data-analytics system.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.810
Google supports data
at petabyte scale

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:00.460
securely, with our
best-in-class data analytics

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.020
and machine-learning
capabilities

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:05.695
to inform real-time decisions
and to operate roadways.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.970
For almost two
decades, Google has

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.360
been advancing the state of
the art for data analytics,

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.040
starting with our
initial research paper

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.650
on the Google File
System in 2002,

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.960
going to our map-produced
research in 2004,

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.852
and our NoSQL database
BigTable research in 2005.

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.560
We've been advancing
the state of the art

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:32.390
and creating a revolution that
the open-source community has

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:36.470
taken and, around that, have
built what many would say

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:42.760
is the best-in-class
data-analytic system in Hadoop

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.883
and in Apache HBase.

00:06:44.883 --> 00:06:47.300
But Google didn't just stop
doing our research engineering

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.290
in these areas.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:52.890
We've continued to advance
the technology, year on year,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:55.260
to help power all
of our services

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.870
that we bring to you,
for example, Colossus,

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:00.750
which is our
next-generation file system,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:05.540
or Dremel, which is our
scalable SQL engine,

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.400
to Flume and
Millwheel, which are

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.780
our next-generation
data-processing pipelines,

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.830
and to today, where we're using
TensorFlow for scalable machine

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.410
learning.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:17.510
And while we're using
these for our own services,

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.040
we've also made them
available to you, to CDOT,

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.180
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.400
So you can take advantage
of these same services that

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.950
have been proven
inside Google for years

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:29.170
to now power your products,
and your applications,

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.545
and your services that you're
bringing to your customers.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:37.290
And like I said, this
infrastructure powers Google's

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:42.810
8-billion-plus user services,
Google Search, Chrome,

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:46.596
Google Maps, YouTube,
Gmail, Google Drive.

00:07:46.596 --> 00:07:48.060
But let's pause for a minute.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.220
Let's actually think
about what these systems

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:51.930
are at their core.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.810
And are they actually what they
appear to be on their face?

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:59.630
Is Gmail just a system for
sending and receiving email?

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.110
Is YouTube just a video
content delivery system?

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.760
Or is Android just a
mobile operating system?

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.160
What I'd argue is that
these are actually

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:12.600
massive globally-scaled
analytical engines

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.950
that leverage machine
learning to bring personalized

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:19.660
results to our users, when
and where they need them.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.530
Google Cloud delivers
data analysis

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:27.350
through industry-leading
analytics and ML,

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:30.058
when and where you need them,
with security and privacy

00:08:30.058 --> 00:08:31.350
in the forefront of our design.

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.710
We have four key
design principles

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:40.150
for our data-analytics stack.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.760
And these are top of
mind for our customers.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.970
First, our serverless data
analytics infrastructure,

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:48.290
our leadership in that space--

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.670
we are designing a
data-analytics stack,

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.870
or we have designed a
data-analytics stack

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.060
for the public cloud paradigm.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.520
We're not lifting and shifting
traditional on-premise

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.750
architectures into the cloud.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:02.240
This enables our
customers, all of you,

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.720
CDOT, to instead of focusing
on traditional infrastructure

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.600
like they normally would do,
to focus on analytics, which

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.460
is really what we
want is to focus

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:15.710
on the analysis of the
data that we're collecting.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:18.080
We're building and offering
a comprehensive set

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.480
of core data-analytics
workloads,

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:22.520
from data warehousing,
to streaming data

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:28.370
analytics, to business
intelligence, to AI and ML.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:37.280
Unlike vendors with one-off
solutions, this allows--

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:40.250
our approach to this is
a much more holistic view

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:43.150
of a data-analytics platform.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:45.750
We also let our
customers operationalize

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.440
machine learning end-to-end.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:52.150
ML training and prediction is
just one piece of the puzzle.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:53.910
But offering up a
seamless ability

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.750
to collect, store,
transform, process,

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:03.055
and prepare data for ML gives
us an end-to-end solution.

00:10:03.055 --> 00:10:10.800
We are deliberate in
making our ML stack simpler

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.050
for organizations
to adopt, so they

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:19.210
can derive information from
their ML initiatives faster.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:22.620
And to finish off, we know this.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:27.420
Google has proven the usefulness
of this infrastructure

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.280
through the services
that we offer.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.020
We're at the forefront
of data analytics

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.320
and ML innovation for
the past two decades.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.240
And the solutions
and frameworks are

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.625
available to our customers
today, who are now

00:10:38.625 --> 00:10:41.250
adopting this and running their
mission-critical data analytics

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.360
apps on top of Google Cloud.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:46.220
But it's not just
the cloud technology

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:48.450
that Google offers
that's important here.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.000
It's also our history
and innovation

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.630
in the transportation space.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.540
Organizations need
reliable, real-time data,

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:59.810
within user-friendly
views, to empower

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:05.190
employees to respond to
customers' demands and needs.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:08.340
Google Maps offers
visualization, navigation,

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:13.940
and analytics to help lean
enterprises chart new territory

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:19.380
and to build transformative,
new opportunities.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.750
Nobody understands
cities and what

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.470
happens within them, and
the people who live there.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:29.450
Waze offers up a
data-exchange partnership,

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:33.030
where we provide a
publicly-available incident

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:36.900
and slow-down data to
our government partners.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:40.050
Government's going to take that
data to both help them respond

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:43.230
to accidents more
effectively, and also

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:49.750
analyze congestion to find where
they can make improvements.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:54.850
Driving today is not as safe
or as easy as we would like.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.450
And Waymo-- their
mission is to make

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.250
it safe and easy for
people and things

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.070
to move around the world.

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:05.970
They're building a completely
self-driving technology

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:09.257
that will improve mobility
by giving people the freedom

00:12:09.257 --> 00:12:09.840
to get around.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.290
And it helps save thousands of
lives that are, today, being

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.120
lost to accidents.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.790
So let's get into
DAISy and what it is.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:19.050
So first the acronym--

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:23.060
Data Analytics
Intelligence System.

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:24.740
There is a flurry
of activity at CDOT,

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.740
which is intended to improve
the acquisition, processing,

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:30.770
management, curation, and
quality of the data set

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:32.610
CDOT has.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:34.110
Tackling those
fundamental problems

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:38.160
will allow CDOT to enhance
the utilization of data,

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.320
create applications,
and solve use cases that

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.230
are becoming critical in
the rapidly transforming

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.427
transportation space.

00:12:45.427 --> 00:12:47.010
Wes Maurer, who
unfortunately couldn't

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:50.760
be here, the head of policy
and innovation at CDOT,

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.500
along with the mobility
operations team,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:56.430
is leading the charge for
adopting cloud computing

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.490
and real-time data
processing, with the goal

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:01.710
of enabling data
analytics and modeling

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.480
capabilities across CDOT.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.540
The DAISy platform
has the potential

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:09.760
to solve many use-cases,
delivering better decision

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:14.340
to support and moving us closer
to data-driven decision-making.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:20.520
There are many use cases that--

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.520
transportation use cases--
that CDOT focuses on.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.180
And this is just a quick sample.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:27.530
For example, transportation
planning, winter operations,

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:31.040
or situational awareness.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:32.870
These use cases are
supported by a myriad

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.480
of disconnected
systems, such as SAP,

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.320
which has all budgeting
and financial information,

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:43.220
SalesForce, which holds
construction and project

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:50.600
details, to roadway sensors for
real-time mobile operations.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:52.490
DAISy is a data
services and systems

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:56.060
layer which aligns the use
cases and the requirements

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.420
with transformed, enriched,
and coalesced data

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:03.290
from source systems,
and empowering end users

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:06.940
to achieve CDOT's key
performance measures.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.300
DAISy will also be
the hub for enabling

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.600
new applications across
the DOT by delivering

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:15.995
historical and real-time
data and also leveraging

00:14:15.995 --> 00:14:18.610
AI and ML for
predictive analytics.

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:24.360
So with all this in
mind, Google capabilities

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:30.200
with data ingestion,
exploration, and data science,

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:33.680
preparation of processing
and ML training, tuning,

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.660
and predictions,
this architecture

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:38.960
enables DAISy to become
a serverless analytics

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.890
engine for a complete
end-to-end lifecycle, delivering

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.600
CDOT's mission to bring
intelligence and insights

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.360
to the transportation space, to
their transportation network.

00:14:49.475 --> 00:14:50.850
So now let's
introduce Zef, who's

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:54.300
going to come in and talk--
do a deeper dive into building

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:56.524
DAISy with data.

00:14:56.524 --> 00:15:00.003
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:06.840
ZEF DELGADILLO: Thanks, Chris.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:09.600
My name is Zef Delgadillo, the
technical lead on the DAISy

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.890
project, working with
Google Cloud and Cloudreach.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:16.428
So when we kicked off this
project several months ago,

00:15:16.428 --> 00:15:18.970
the first thing we had to do is
assess all the different data

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.550
sets that were
available at CDOT,

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:24.080
across their entire
technology ecosystem.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:25.580
The first thing we
really discovered

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.900
is that there's kind of two
major categories of data

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.870
for a DOT or for transportation.

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:34.723
That's around your geospatial
data or streaming video data.

00:15:34.723 --> 00:15:37.140
And Andrew's going to talk a
lot about the video streaming

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:37.900
data in a second.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.800
But I'm going to go into
some of the geospatial data

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.720
and some of the
capabilities we're

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.190
unlocking by using that data.

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:46.960
Geospatial data is
really just any type

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.340
of data that has a
geographic component to it,

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:54.330
so a set of coordinates
associated with that data.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:57.530
So we then broke that data down
into a few different major data

00:15:57.530 --> 00:16:01.650
domains that are critical
to a DOT operation.

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:05.300
And that's really around
speed, weather, and incidents.

00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:08.300
So speed-- how fast our
vehicle is traveling relative

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:12.350
to speed limit and moving
averages, and then vehicle

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:14.870
volume on the highway system.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:16.370
Right now, that's
primarily detected

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:19.007
using some side-fire
radars that are fairly old.

00:16:19.007 --> 00:16:20.840
Some of that data that
often comes out of it

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:24.590
is buggy or inadequate.

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:28.128
Weather data-- weather
data right now, for CDOT,

00:16:28.128 --> 00:16:30.170
is collected using these
road weather information

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:33.330
system devices that also
live alongside the roadway.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:34.880
So this becomes
kind of an IoT play

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.290
as we start getting into this.

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:40.810
The third data domain
is around incident data.

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:44.430
So basically, any kind of
traffic incident that gets

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:48.810
logged by the DOT operators
into their traffic-management

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:50.610
system.

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:53.125
So those are what we're
calling the legacy data sets.

00:16:53.125 --> 00:16:54.500
One of the things
we wanted to do

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:55.875
is enrich that
with some new data

00:16:55.875 --> 00:16:57.833
so that we can get
additional value out

00:16:57.833 --> 00:16:59.500
of data sets that are
available publicly

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:02.090
or through different
organizations.

00:17:02.090 --> 00:17:06.089
So for speed data, we
brought in data from Waze.

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:09.069
Waze is crowdsource
data that we can

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:11.770
track when jams are
starting to build up

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.329
on the highway systems.

00:17:13.329 --> 00:17:15.819
And that gives us a better
picture of the roadway health

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:16.810
according to speed.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.502
So right now, CDOT
is detecting speed

00:17:18.502 --> 00:17:19.960
using an algorithm
that really only

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.890
takes into account the data
coming from side-fire radars.

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:26.619
So by bringing in Waze data,
we can get additional value out

00:17:26.619 --> 00:17:27.970
of that.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.220
Weather data-- this is a
really cool data set produced

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:32.220
by the National Center
for Atmospheric Research,

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:33.560
called Pikalert.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.580
Pikalert has these vehicles
traveling up and down

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.060
major trucking routes in
Colorado, particularly I-70,

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:41.480
and it detects road conditions.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:45.330
So if there's an icy road
or there's snow on the road,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.140
you'll get back data
around snow depth

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:50.280
or whether that road
needs to be treated.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:55.320
Incidents-- so Waze is another
great data set for incidents.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:57.990
Crowdsource accidents that
people are putting in can be,

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:00.200
now, added in addition
to the DOT events

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:01.200
that are getting logged.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:05.860
So we wanted to make this
data a little bit more

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.570
accessible to DOT users.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:10.390
Chris mentioned that this
data existed in a lot of silos

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:11.530
all over the DOT.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:14.380
It was in Salesforce, SAP.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:17.518
We had to do this massive
Oracle Data Warehouse migration.

00:18:17.518 --> 00:18:19.810
So we basically brought in
terabytes of historical data

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:22.777
into Google Cloud BigQuery.

00:18:22.777 --> 00:18:24.610
And we set up this sort
of abstraction layer

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:27.910
on top, to make it a little
bit more accessible to someone,

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:30.880
maybe, who has a lot of domain
expertise in the transportation

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.010
space, but doesn't
want to be bogged down

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:35.620
with trying to remember what
table to join on to derive

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.870
value out of those data sets.

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.960
So we created simple,
business-friendly entities

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:44.030
at the top view layer, in the
form of views in BigQuery,

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:46.960
and created a data
set called DAISyMart.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:49.750
With that, we're using a
business-intelligence tool,

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:53.293
Looker, that utilizes these same
entities to kind of democratize

00:18:53.293 --> 00:18:54.460
this data a little bit more.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:19:01.148
So here's kind of a simplified
view of our architecture.

00:19:01.148 --> 00:19:03.190
Really, it's all kind of
centered around Pub/Sub.

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:06.458
Pub/Sub enables us to,
basically, use streaming data

00:19:06.458 --> 00:19:08.000
across all these
different data sets.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.398
So even in cases where we're
batching in data-- so Waze,

00:19:10.398 --> 00:19:12.940
we might be reaching out every
five minutes to get the latest

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:13.937
Waze data--

00:19:13.937 --> 00:19:15.520
we could still
translate it to Pub/Sub

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.257
and have a series
of Pub/Sub topics

00:19:18.257 --> 00:19:20.590
that are kind of like-- you
can think of them as spigots

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:22.840
you would attach a hose
to, to pull in that data.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:26.080
So it enables any, really,
application or user

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:30.130
to pull in data from Waze
or data from the IoT devices

00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:33.320
and build out their
applications with it.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:37.135
We're pushing all that data
into BigQuery and BigTable.

00:19:37.135 --> 00:19:38.510
And on the right,
you can kind of

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.332
see how we're looking at DAISy.

00:19:40.332 --> 00:19:42.290
And it's sort of like a
three-pronged platform.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:44.420
You have event-driven
data in Pub/Sub,

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:48.650
like I mentioned before, near
real-time data in Google Cloud

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:51.050
BigTable, and the
analytics layer that

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:52.400
uses BigQuery and Looker.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:59.690
Geospatial data-- so I mentioned
before that geospatial data

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:00.940
is-- its kind of special.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:02.520
It's kind of important to CDOT.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:05.480
And we wanted to use some
kind of best-in-class tools

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:08.300
to sort and analyze that data.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.850
So GeoMesa is our first one.

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:13.040
GeoMesa is an open-source
tool for, basically,

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:16.430
massive geospatial
querying and ingestion.

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:18.080
It stores in Google
Cloud BigTable,

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:20.665
and it does all its
processing in Apache Spark.

00:20:20.665 --> 00:20:23.090
So you can run it on
a Dataproc cluster.

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:26.030
It transforms indexes and then
stores that geographic data

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:26.850
for rapid access.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:29.180
So you can query,
basically, on either time

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:30.380
or some kind of geography.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.890
You could pick like a
window of space on a map

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:36.686
and then get all of the data
available through there.

00:20:36.686 --> 00:20:38.570
GeoServer is the other
tool we're using.

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:42.605
And that's our mechanism to
share that geospatial data.

00:20:42.605 --> 00:20:45.470
So it uses GeoMesa as
it's back-end data store.

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:47.635
We're running it on
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:20:47.635 --> 00:20:49.010
and it publishes
data to, really,

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:52.160
any kind of geographic data
standard, including GeoJSON.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:56.690
GeoJSON has kind of emerged as
a de facto standard for sharing

00:20:56.690 --> 00:20:58.668
geographic data over the web.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.380
So a couple use cases that,
after a few months, really,

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:08.490
we were able to unlock using the
computational power of Google

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:10.550
Cloud BigQuery.

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:13.318
So CDOT has its concept
of a road segment.

00:21:13.318 --> 00:21:15.110
And a road segment is
really just a stretch

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:18.220
of road between two points
going in a single direction.

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.810
And that road segment has a
set of geographic coordinates

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:23.020
associated with it.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:24.750
So using Google
Cloud BigQuery, we

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:28.800
can basically attach these
Waze accidents, or incidents,

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.310
off to the road segments.

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:33.120
So when you're querying, or
somebody who-- a transportation

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:36.600
planner is querying on the
health of that road segment,

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:38.850
not only do you get the
events that the DOT logged,

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:40.920
you also get the Waze accidents.

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:42.510
So this is a really
powerful, new tool

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:46.620
that we can unlock using the
computational power of Google

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.070
Cloud BigQuery, that using
their on-prem data warehouse

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:50.445
just wouldn't have
been possible.

00:21:54.378 --> 00:21:56.170
Enabling situational
awareness and replay--

00:21:56.170 --> 00:21:57.837
so this concept of
situational awareness

00:21:57.837 --> 00:22:00.820
is understanding what's
going on in the DOT.

00:22:00.820 --> 00:22:03.380
So if you ever go to a
DOT operation center,

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:06.640
they've got all these maps
and all these different camera

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.060
views all over the place.

00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:11.830
And they're toggling between
Waze and Google Maps--

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:13.820
their own mapping systems--

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.820
to understand what's going on.

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:18.700
So with the architecture
that we built,

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:21.610
we're enabling this
mechanism to build out

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:23.845
some kind of situational
awareness layer.

00:22:23.845 --> 00:22:27.460
And on top of that, we can
now, using the platform,

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:30.360
replay an existing event.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.750
So a few weeks ago, there was
this big storm in Colorado.

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:35.630
They called it a bomb cyclone.

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:40.520
And we were able to capture that
data using our DAISy platform.

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:43.070
And it gives us the opportunity
to replay that event

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:45.620
and watch how all these
different layers interplayed,

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:48.343
so that a transportation planner
can go back, review an event,

00:22:48.343 --> 00:22:49.760
and understand how
they can better

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.810
prepare for it in the future.

00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:55.430
Kepler.gl is an
open-source mapping tool

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:58.663
produced by Uber, which I think
is pretty cool that there's

00:22:58.663 --> 00:23:00.080
this sort of
disruptive technology

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:04.100
that we can now reapply to
the DOT, which has been around

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:05.820
forever.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.150
So I'm using Kepler as
just a mechanism to demo

00:23:08.150 --> 00:23:12.830
out this GeoServer, GeoMesa
data-processing system

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:15.172
that we've set up here.

00:23:15.172 --> 00:23:16.505
So this is kind of a cool video.

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:21.627
We're basically replaying
all of the Waze accidents

00:23:21.627 --> 00:23:23.210
that occurred during
the bomb cyclone.

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:24.627
You can see that
they kind of just

00:23:24.627 --> 00:23:27.890
dip to nothing here because so
few people were on the roads.

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:30.740
But this sort of gives
an interactive way

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:35.690
of a user to take a look
at the roadway health

00:23:35.690 --> 00:23:39.678
and create geospatial
visualizations, which

00:23:39.678 --> 00:23:41.720
is a capability that would
not have been possible

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.850
in the previous ecosystem.

00:23:47.353 --> 00:23:48.520
A similar thing with speed--

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:49.780
so we can kind of
detect highway speeds.

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:51.580
It's going to go
really red in a second.

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:53.230
And the cool thing
is that we're really

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:55.810
animating millions of
entities right here that we're

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:57.160
pulling in.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:00.640
From GeoServer, we're just
getting a GeoJSON endpoint,

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:03.780
pushing it into
kepler.gl, that Uber tool,

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.640
and then we can immediately
animate and then

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:08.117
replay across a historical set.

00:24:08.117 --> 00:24:09.700
So this really is
just a demonstration

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:13.000
of the future capabilities
that we're trying to unlock.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.890
By building out this
underlying platform

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:17.953
and that infrastructure, we have
a lot of really powerful tools

00:24:17.953 --> 00:24:18.620
at our disposal.

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:25.547
With that, I'm going to
pass it off to Andrew.

00:24:25.547 --> 00:24:27.630
I'm going to be at the
Google Public Sector booth,

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:29.550
and I'm happy to go through
a demonstration of Kepler

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.290
in a little more detail
if you'd like to see it.

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:31.790
Thanks.

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:34.972
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:38.332 --> 00:24:39.540
ANDREW SCHWARTZ: Thanks, Zef.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:40.180
Hi, everyone.

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:41.638
My name is Andrew
Schwartz, and I'm

00:24:41.638 --> 00:24:44.740
a product manager for Video
AI, here at Google Cloud.

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:48.750
At Google Cloud AI, we have the
mission to empower enterprises

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:52.365
to transform their
business with AI.

00:24:52.365 --> 00:24:56.990
For CDOT, video is a great
example of this transformation.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:59.710
CDOT currently has hundreds
of roadside cameras collecting

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:02.020
valuable information every day.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.270
However, this information
has not been fully utilized,

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:08.680
as it has been too difficult to
reliably and scalably analyze

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:11.810
these videos and collect
the data from them.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:14.260
However, that is why
CDOT decided to leverage

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:17.200
the power of Google Cloud AI,
to help them bring intelligence

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:20.080
to his legacy camera systems,
without the need for replacing

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.720
them for higher-resolution
and higher processing power

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:25.146
cameras.

00:25:25.146 --> 00:25:28.360
At Google Cloud AI, we offer
two types of AI building blocks

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:30.040
to help customers build--

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:33.540
or add human-like capabilities
to their products.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:36.490
The first I'd like to talk
about is the pre-trained APIs.

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:39.150
The pre-trained APIs allow
customers to leverage Google

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:41.983
predefined data sets to
meet common use cases

00:25:41.983 --> 00:25:44.400
and fully trained models based
on the latest breakthroughs

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.310
in AI technology and research.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:49.570
The second is AutoML.

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:52.570
Even for expert data scientists,
it's quite difficult for them

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:55.600
to train and program
custom computer-vision,

00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:59.650
machine-learning models, which
is why we created AutoML.

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:04.940
AutoML enables even
non-programmers to leverage

00:26:04.940 --> 00:26:08.248
a simple user interface
to create custom models.

00:26:08.248 --> 00:26:09.790
All they have to do
is upload labeled

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:13.130
data sets, and then with a
simple click of a button,

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:15.440
train a custom
computer-vision model fit

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:18.010
to their specific domain,
or specific use case,

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:22.010
and deploy it to production.

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:23.750
Today, building upon
this, we're really

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:25.250
excited to announce
that we've added

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:28.280
additional functionality to our
Video Intelligence streaming--

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:32.860
Video Intelligence APIs in the
form of streaming capabilities.

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:35.200
Now enterprises
have the flexibility

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:37.420
to not only process
large video archives that

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.400
are stored in Google
Cloud Storage,

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:42.130
but also to analyze
videos in real time

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.900
and get actionable
insights right on the spot.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.450
With live streaming support,
we have initial support

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:52.920
for live streaming protocols,
such as HLS, RTMP, and RTSP,

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.600
initially.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.820
So with the power of
the pre-trained models

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:01.210
and the streaming API, CDOT
is able to bring intelligence

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:03.440
to their existing camera feeds.

00:27:03.440 --> 00:27:08.180
For instance, CDOT leverages our
pre-trained object-tracking API

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:09.962
to extract valuable
information and detect

00:27:09.962 --> 00:27:12.170
different types of vehicles
and track their movements

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:14.440
throughout the day.

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:17.140
This information helps them
understand traffic patterns,

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:20.730
as well as identify trends
and make decisions off of it.

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:23.820
Or they can leverage our
label detection in AutoML

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:27.308
to monitor road conditions, to
identify whether it's snowing

00:27:27.308 --> 00:27:29.850
or whether there's snow on the
road, which can further enrich

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:33.430
weather data as part of DAISy.

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:35.910
The streaming API allows
customers to leverage

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.220
or harness the power of a
cloud to do video processing

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:39.560
at scale.

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:41.950
But it gives them the
flexibility to take the results

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:45.600
and either process it
further on Google Cloud,

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:47.970
or to view the
results on-premise,

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:50.640
in their application.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:52.790
To help you get started
and easily connected

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:54.680
to Google Cloud
streaming API, we

00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:57.710
provide AI streamer, which
is our ingestion library,

00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:01.217
and a set of sample code to
easily process-- or chunk

00:28:01.217 --> 00:28:03.050
the videos and send it
to the cloud for you.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:07.980
Here, we provide a simple
architecture-- or a sample

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:10.530
architecture-- for how you
might connect and utilize

00:28:10.530 --> 00:28:12.550
the streaming API.

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:15.420
AI Streamer takes in the
video feeds and sends it

00:28:15.420 --> 00:28:19.327
to the cloud for
analytics and storage.

00:28:19.327 --> 00:28:21.910
With this, you can then run the
pre-trained Video Intelligence

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:24.370
APIs to extract
valuable information

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:27.590
from your video feeds and then
pipe the results in the cloud

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:29.350
Pub/Sub, where that
information can then

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:32.060
be passed to your
application in the cloud,

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:36.210
such as it is for DAISy, or back
to your application on site,

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:37.525
for a visualization.

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.090
So what does this all mean?

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:45.840
It means that when you construct
a valuable video information

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:50.460
and combine it with DAISy
data, other DAISy data sources,

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.160
you can solve real
transportation problems.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:54.600
You can reduce costs.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:56.550
You can optimize roads.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.130
We can help you get
home quicker from work.

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:01.525
We can make the
roads safer for you

00:29:01.525 --> 00:29:03.650
and to have you spend more
time doing what you love

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:07.043
and less time spent on the road.

00:29:07.043 --> 00:29:07.543
OK.

00:29:07.543 --> 00:29:11.470
Next, we'll show a quick demo of
one of our object-tracking APIs

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:11.980
running--

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:15.827
or pre-trained models-- running
on one of DAISy camera feeds.

00:29:15.827 --> 00:29:17.410
This is pre-recorded,
but if you would

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:18.903
like to see a
live-streaming demo,

00:29:18.903 --> 00:29:21.070
you can come by the Public
Sector booth later today,

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:24.160
or later this week, and see,
actually, a live feed being

00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:26.890
ingested, as well as the
bounding boxes overlaid

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:29.250
for the different videos.

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:32.270
So with this, you can track the
movements of different vehicles

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:35.330
crossing through an
intersection and keep these data

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:38.030
or store them as part of DAISy.

00:29:38.030 --> 00:29:40.490
Next, Chris will
actually tell you

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:42.620
some information regarding
this, around how much

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:45.720
data is actually being
extracted from these videos,

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:48.049
even with the small number
of cameras, currently.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:29:59.710
CHRISTOPHER HAAS:
Thanks, Andrew.

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:04.780
So to pivot on what Andrew
talked about with the Video

00:30:04.780 --> 00:30:08.260
Intelligence streaming
API, a few weeks ago,

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:10.990
we deployed video-streaming
infrastructure

00:30:10.990 --> 00:30:14.140
from CDOT's
on-premise data center

00:30:14.140 --> 00:30:19.640
to Google Cloud,
the DAISy platform.

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:21.390
And we've been collecting
data ever since,

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:23.567
on only four cameras.

00:30:23.567 --> 00:30:25.650
So I want to quickly just
give a view of how we're

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:28.450
using this metadata-- in
addition to the bounding box,

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:31.800
the video stream that you saw,
how we're using this metadata

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:36.750
to gain further insights that
CDOT originally did not have.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:42.990
So CDOT has roughly 488-some
odd cameras connected

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:46.710
to their network, in addition
to thousands of others that

00:30:46.710 --> 00:30:50.230
are connected over cellular.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:52.000
These cameras are
all legacy devices.

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:53.000
They're not intelligent.

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:54.910
They're not giving data out.

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:59.790
They're just live streaming so
that an operator can view it.

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:03.110
On the right-hand side,
one of the cameras

00:31:03.110 --> 00:31:06.720
happens to be in South
of Denver, on I-25.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:09.360
And that's a still
image of that camera.

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:11.940
We've been extracting data
around vehicle counts,

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:16.850
both total by hour, by day,
and averages by weekday.

00:31:16.850 --> 00:31:19.950
And you can see each of
those heat maps, accordingly.

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:25.070
And now, we can take these
non-intelligent devices,

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:27.890
extract data from
them, and have insights

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:30.898
into what is visible
in that live stream.

00:31:30.898 --> 00:31:33.190
And this would correlate--
if you look in the heat maps

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:36.740
there, it correlates to
increases in traffic volume

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.860
throughout the day, aligning
with what we would normally

00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:42.307
know as our commute times.

00:31:42.307 --> 00:31:44.640
So to give you an idea of the
volume we're operating out

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:46.410
here-- so four cameras.

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:48.540
They've been
operating for 13 days.

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:50.820
And over that time,
we've ingested

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:54.360
over 60 million data
points for labels

00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:56.730
and over 21 million objects.

00:31:56.730 --> 00:31:58.860
Now, granted, not
all of this data

00:31:58.860 --> 00:32:01.200
is necessarily useful
for every use case.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.870
But just think, you went
from almost zero data

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:06.690
points, besides what a human
would see out of a camera,

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:08.430
to, now, ingesting
all of this data

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:11.250
into a scalable data
warehouse in BigQuery.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:14.010
So now we can analyze it
both real-time, as well as

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:17.520
historical.

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:21.270
So to give you a view
of where we're going--

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:23.080
where we've been and
where we're going--

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:26.340
here's a tentative roadmap
for what this partnership is

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:28.857
going to go forward with.

00:32:28.857 --> 00:32:30.690
So starting in March,
you see at the bottom,

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:34.110
we did our executive review
that closed out phase I.

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:36.810
For phase I, we delivered
a technical systems plan

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:38.760
for the DAISy system.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.740
We built out core
infrastructure for DAISy,

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:44.530
as well as multiple
data-integration pipelines.

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:47.410
We also did-- we're
doing a full sync of all

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:49.750
their historical data from
their Oracle Data Warehouse,

00:32:49.750 --> 00:32:52.280
and we have a migration
plan for that as well.

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:54.070
We also delivered a
technical system plan

00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:57.850
for a modernized Colorado
traffic-management system.

00:32:57.850 --> 00:33:00.320
Now going forward, we're
looking to continue building out

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:02.360
more of the core
system, build out

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:03.920
multiple data-integration
pipelines

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:05.240
beyond what we have today.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:06.698
And if you remember
the slide back,

00:33:06.698 --> 00:33:08.870
we had all those myriad
disconnected systems.

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:12.380
There's lots of those that we
need to build pipelines and get

00:33:12.380 --> 00:33:13.970
into DAISy.

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:15.290
So we'll continue to do that.

00:33:15.290 --> 00:33:17.750
We'll do a full migration of
the Oracle Data Warehouse.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:20.270
We're building multiple
real-time visualization

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:24.950
interfaces, such as the example
that Zef showed with Kepler.

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:27.500
We're also going
to explore doing

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:29.300
the development for
a modernized Colorado

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:33.230
traffic-management system,
and modernizing and hosting

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:35.510
their COTrip.org
public-facing website,

00:33:35.510 --> 00:33:40.280
in GCP, being powered
by real-time modern APIs

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:42.250
from DAISy.

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:43.810
We're also going
to explore building

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:47.470
a data-discovery platform, in
a real-time data hub that CDOT

00:33:47.470 --> 00:33:51.190
will use to, both, do
data analytics broadly

00:33:51.190 --> 00:33:55.784
across the DOT, as well as
new application development.

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:00.790
So in closing, CDOT
and Google have

00:34:00.790 --> 00:34:04.350
partnered to build DAISy,
which will aid CDOT

00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:07.250
in delivering on their
key performance measures.

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:12.600
For example, improved
safety and mobility,

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:16.020
increased social equity,
highly-responsive traffic

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:19.830
operations, and collaborative
data and information sharing

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:22.278
and communications.

00:34:22.278 --> 00:34:24.070
So right before I end,
just I'll reiterate,

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:26.190
if you want to see live
demos of the technology

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:30.449
we've shown, go to the industry
solutions area, and the Public

00:34:30.449 --> 00:34:31.760
Sector booth, specifically.

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:35.909
We have multiple live
demos that you can look at.

00:34:35.909 --> 00:34:39.310
And also, you can ask questions
to members of the DAISy team.

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:41.515
So thank you for
attending the session.

00:34:41.515 --> 00:34:42.390
I hope it was useful.

00:34:42.390 --> 00:34:42.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:46.040
